We can repeat the same derivation for problem ([2](#S1.E2 "(2) ‣ 1.2 Contribution ‣ 1 Introduction and Related Work ‣ Network Classifiers with Output Smoothing"))-([4](#S1.E4 "(4) ‣ 1.2 Contribution ‣ 1 Introduction and Related Work ‣ Network Classifiers with Output Smoothing")), leading to Algorithm [2](#alg2 "Algorithm 2 ‣ 2 Distributed Classification Algorithms ‣ Network Classifiers with Output Smoothing"). In this implementation, each agent estimates its label by using the local averaging structure ([4](#S1.E4 "(4) ‣ 1.2 Contribution ‣ 1 Introduction and Related Work ‣ Network Classifiers with Output Smoothing")). The vector gk,n refers to a stochastic approximation for the loss term, and is used to update wk,i−1 to wk,i. Again, once the classifiers are trained, and during testing, the agents employ ([4](#S1.E4 "(4) ‣ 1.2 Contribution ‣ 1 Introduction and Related Work ‣ Network Classifiers with Output Smoothing")) to estimate their labels. This second implementation is more suitable for scenarios where the entire network acts as a cluster with all agents observing features from the same class. We will illustrate these effects in the simulation section.





