Figure 3:  Average testing errors on simulated data.


Next, we consider a scenario where one part of the network is observing data from one class while another part is observing data from another class, as shown in Fig. [4](#S4.F4 "Figure 4 ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ Network Classifiers with Output Smoothing"). We observe similar results as in the previous case, except that now the cooperative algorithms do not reach zero testing errors, but they still outperform the non-cooperative algorithm.



![](https://media.arxiv-vanity.com/render-output/7816159/x4.png)
Figure 4: Network structure with non-uniform labels used in the simulations.


Finally, we consider a real weather dataset consisting of a collection of daily recordings over 13 years, taken from K=139 sensors located in different weather stations across the United States [[23](#bib.bib23)]. A 6-nearest neighbour distance graph was created to represent the network of sensors. Each sensor has a set of N=3288 samples consisting of features indicating weather there is rain or not, and a feature vector of size M=5. In this experiment, we only run Algorithm [1](#alg1 "Algorithm 1 ‣ 2 Distributed Classification Algorithms ‣ Network Classifiers with Output Smoothing") and compare it to the non-cooperative case, since this data does not fall under the assumptions made for the second algorithm (neighbouring agents may observe different labels over time). We set the step size to μ=3×10−4, and the regularizer parameters η=1×10−5 and ρ=0.3. The non-cooperative solution achieved an average testing error of 0.2001, while Algorithm [1](#alg1 "Algorithm 1 ‣ 2 Distributed Classification Algorithms ‣ Network Classifiers with Output Smoothing") achieved 0.1851. In Fig. [5](#S4.F5 "Figure 5 ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ Network Classifiers with Output Smoothing"), for the 1st of July, 2013, we represent the true labeled graph on the left and the predicted one on the right. Specifically, for this day, the testing error achieved was 0.1079.






